            {
              "id": "139b57a1-f0a1-3c12-9979-b06cb4d72458",
              "name": "\"Non-Literal Path File Read Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nNon-Literal Path File Read Vulnerability in C# refers to a situation where an application uses input from an upstream component to specify the pathname of a file to be read, but it does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes special elements within the pathname that can cause the pathname to resolve to a location that is outside of the restricted directory.\n\nThis can allow an attacker to read sensitive data or files from the system, leading to information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Always validate user input: Ensure that the file path does not contain any special characters or sequences that could potentially change the intended path of the file.\n- Use a whitelist of acceptable inputs: This can prevent an attacker from accessing unauthorized files.\n- Limit permissions: Ensure that the application runs with the least privileges necessary. This can limit the potential damage if an attacker is able to exploit this vulnerability.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to fix the vulnerability in the provided code:\n\n```csharp\npublic string ReadAllText(string path)\n{\n    if (Path.IsPathRooted(path))\n    {\n        throw new ArgumentException(\"Absolute paths are not allowed\", nameof(path));\n    }\n\n    string fullPath = Path.GetFullPath(Path.Combine(AppDomain.CurrentDomain.BaseDirectory, path));\n\n    if (!fullPath.StartsWith(AppDomain.CurrentDomain.BaseDirectory, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))\n    {\n        throw new ArgumentException(\"Path traversal is not allowed\", nameof(path));\n    }\n\n    return File.ReadAllText(fullPath);\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks if the path is rooted (absolute) and if it is, it throws an exception. It also checks if the full path starts with the base directory of the application and if it doesn't, it throws an exception. This prevents path traversal attacks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- System\n- System.IO\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-23: Relative Path Traversal](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/23.html)"
              },
