            {
              "id": "27aae181-1117-39da-8b88-df3aea49ff73",
              "name": "\"Possible Path Traversal: Non-Literal File Read Vulnerability\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Possible Path Traversal: Non-Literal File Read Vulnerability\" is a security flaw that occurs when an application uses user-supplied input to read a file without properly validating the input. This can allow an attacker to read files that they should not have access to, by using special characters such as \"../\" to traverse to directories that are outside of the intended directory.\n\nIn C#, this vulnerability can occur when using the `StreamReader` class to read a file, if the stream is created from a path that includes user-supplied input.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate user-supplied input before using it to read a file. This can include checking that the input does not contain special characters or sequences that could be used to traverse to unintended directories, and verifying that the resulting path is within the intended directory.\n\nYou should also consider using a safe method for reading files that automatically handles these checks, such as the `File.ReadLines` method in the `System.IO` namespace.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how you might modify the code to mitigate this vulnerability:\n\n```csharp\nstring userSuppliedPath = GetUserSuppliedPath();\n\n// Validate the user-supplied path.\nif (!IsValidPath(userSuppliedPath))\n{\n    throw new SecurityException(\"Invalid path.\");\n}\n\n// Use a safe method to read the file.\nIEnumerable<string> lines = File.ReadLines(userSuppliedPath);\n```\n\nIn this example, `GetUserSuppliedPath` is a method that gets the user-supplied path, and `IsValidPath` is a method that validates the path.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- System.IO\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [OWASP .NET Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DotNet_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-22",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "452f321b-e065-3905-a07d-9bb188690f87",
              "name": "Detected hardcoded cryptographic key/secret vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected hardcoded cryptographic key/secret vulnerability\" is a security flaw that occurs when a developer hardcodes a secret cryptographic key into the application's source code. This is a serious vulnerability because if an attacker gains access to the source code, they can easily retrieve the hardcoded key and use it to decrypt sensitive data, impersonate the application, or perform other malicious activities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `AppKey` is hardcoded as a constant string. If this key is used for cryptographic operations, it poses a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding cryptographic keys or secrets in your source code. Instead, use secure methods to store and retrieve these keys. For instance, you can use secure environment variables, or secure key management systems.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the `AppKey`, you can store it in an environment variable:\n\n```csharp\nprivate static readonly string AppKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(\"APP_KEY\");\n```\n\nIn this case, you would need to set the `APP_KEY` environment variable to your key value before running the application.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798"
                ]
              }
