            {
              "id": "69e43616-f506-3d5e-b176-dba24ec02063",
              "name": "Potential Data Leak: Response Contains Connection String or Password Literal",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Potential Data Leak: Response Contains Connection String or Password Literal\" refers to a situation where sensitive information such as a connection string or password is included in the response data. This can occur when a developer inadvertently includes such information in the response, or when an error message containing such information is returned. This can lead to unauthorized access to the system or data, as an attacker can use this information to gain access.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, developers should ensure that sensitive information is not included in the response data. This can be achieved by:\n\n- Using parameterized queries or prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.\n- Implementing proper error handling to prevent sensitive information from being included in error messages.\n- Encrypting sensitive data before sending it over the network.\n- Using secure coding practices to prevent the inclusion of sensitive information in the code.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet is a batch command, not a C# code. However, if you have sensitive information in your C# code, you should remove it and use secure methods to store and retrieve it. For example, you can use the .NET Secret Manager to store sensitive data during the development process. \n\n```csharp\n// Bad practice\nstring connectionString = \"Server=myServerAddress;Database=myDataBase;User Id=myUsername;Password=myPassword;\";\n\n// Good practice\nstring connectionString = Configuration.GetConnectionString(\"MyDatabase\");\n```\n\nIn the good practice example, the connection string is stored in a configuration file, not in the code. The .NET Secret Manager can be used to store the connection string securely during development.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet is a batch command, not a C# code. Therefore, it does not have any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10-2017 A3-Sensitive Data Exposure](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure)\n- [CWE-200: Information Exposure](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-200",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "security-severity": "5.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200"
                ]
              }
