            {
              "id": "71f08912-b9e7-3ea8-846a-54715c7014c2",
              "name": "Detected Hardcoded Cryptographic Key/Secret Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Hardcoded Cryptographic Key/Secret Vulnerability\" is a security flaw that occurs when a developer hardcodes a secret cryptographic key into the source code. This is a serious vulnerability because if an attacker gains access to the source code, they can easily retrieve the key and use it to decrypt sensitive data, impersonate the application, or perform other malicious activities.\n\nIn the provided C# code, the `SystemRestoreKeyPath` is a hardcoded string that could potentially be used as a cryptographic key or secret. If this string is used for encryption or decryption in the application, it poses a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid hardcoding cryptographic keys or secrets in your source code. Instead, use secure methods to store and retrieve these keys. For example, you can use secure server environments, key management systems, or secure vault services.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of hardcoding the `SystemRestoreKeyPath`, you can store it in a secure location and retrieve it when needed. Here is an example of how you can do this using .NET's built-in configuration system:\n\n```csharp\npublic class Startup\n{\n    public Startup(IConfiguration configuration)\n    {\n        Configuration = configuration;\n    }\n\n    public IConfiguration Configuration { get; }\n\n    // This method gets called by the runtime. Use this method to add services to the container.\n    public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)\n    {\n        services.Configure<SystemRestoreOptions>(Configuration.GetSection(\"SystemRestore\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn the `appsettings.json` file, you can store the `SystemRestoreKeyPath`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"SystemRestore\": {\n    \"KeyPath\": \"SOFTWARE\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows NT\\\\CurrentVersion\\\\SystemRestore\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThen, you can access the `KeyPath` in your code like this:\n\n```csharp\npublic class MyClass\n{\n    private readonly string _systemRestoreKeyPath;\n\n    public MyClass(IOptions<SystemRestoreOptions> options)\n    {\n        _systemRestoreKeyPath = options.Value.KeyPath;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not require any additional library dependencies to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP .NET Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DotNet_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-798",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
                  }
                ],
